          verified owner reviews spanning one to three years of ownership.
          The incline motor and mechanism — the part of this machine doing
          the most unusual work compared to a standard treadmill — showed a
          low long-term complaint rate, which is reassuring given how much
          more mechanically active it is than a flat incline system. Belt
          tracking came up as the most common maintenance item, consistent
          with treadmills generally, and several owners specifically noted
          that the belt needed re-centering somewhat sooner than on flat
          machines they'd previously owned, likely a function of the belt
          experiencing more directional stress across a much wider incline
          range.
        </p>
        <p>
          NordicTrack's warranty coverage — a 10-year frame, 2-year parts,
          and 1-year labor policy on this model — matches the rest of the
          Commercial-tier lineup and is worth registering promptly after
          purchase. Given the incline mechanism's added mechanical
          complexity relative to a flat treadmill, we'd specifically
          recommend keeping the incline motor's moving parts free of dust
          and pet hair, which owners report as a more common issue on
          incline trainers than on flat decks simply because there's more
          exposed mechanism to accumulate debris around.
        </p>

